WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:03.780
Film Courage: Thinking back to something a
mentor said to the two of you individually,

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:08.350
maybe even before you met one another, what
was it?

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:11.809
Or what statement or mantra never left you?

00:00:11.809 --> 00:00:14.340
It could be Ray Bradbury or someone else.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:15.380
Marc Zicree: Sure.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:21.189
Elaine Zicree: It’s a funny thing but what
most affected me was a script doctor, a very

00:00:21.189 --> 00:00:23.239
good script doctor I had.

00:00:23.239 --> 00:00:27.739
And it was a script I’d written and it was
one of my favorite scripts ultimately but

00:00:27.739 --> 00:00:30.359
she said “This is brilliant.

00:00:30.359 --> 00:00:32.210
I absolutely love it!

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:38.370
Now here’s the notes….” but the fact
that she said that I was brilliant and that

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:41.859
she loved it I thought “Well the heck with
a few notes.

00:00:41.859 --> 00:00:43.590
I’m brilliant and I love it!”

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:50.440
So I think crazy flattery…I realize the
weight of allowing somebody to feel special

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:54.609
and be special, it was crazy flattering that
all of those notes seemed like nothing.

00:00:54.609 --> 00:01:01.429
So one of the biggest pieces of advice was
‘Hey, what you’re doing has super value.

00:01:01.429 --> 00:01:03.429
Now here’s the work.’

00:01:03.429 --> 00:01:09.540
And just going on that thing where I was allowed
to have super value at the top of the journey

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:13.400
really got me through it as if it was nothing
(those notes).

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:23.570
Marc Zicree: In terms of me (the mentors)
what they’ve told me…you know…it’s

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:28.480
funny because the mentors who’ve really
guided me have been Rod Serling, Ray Bradbury,

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:35.050
Guillermo del toro and what really inspired
me was the fact that these are guys that come

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:38.940
from enormous enthusiasm, creative enthusiasm.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:43.040
And one thing Ray said to me was don’t look
outside yourself, look within.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:48.320
And it’s funny there was one time when he
and I was sitting together and I said “I

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:50.250
just figured out what business you’re in.”

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:53.890
I said “It’s not writer, you’re in the
Ray Bradbury business.”

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:54.890
And he said “Yes!

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:56.110
That’s exactly right.”

00:01:56.110 --> 00:02:00.600
And so what he was doing was creating something
that no one else could create.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:06.800
And he told me that he wrote every day for
10 years before he a single line, a single

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:08.940
word that was uniquely his.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:14.060
And then one day he sat down and he wrote
the words THE LAKE and he wrote a short story

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:15.570
based on the time when he was a little boy.

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:19.850
When he was eight years old he and a little
girl friend who was seven went swimming in

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:23.930
Lake Michigan and he came out and she never
did (she drowned).

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:27.610
And it was a story of her coming back as a
ghost and they told me that when he finished

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:31.350
the story, tears were streaming down his face
and he knew that he’d written something

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:35.220
that no one else could have written and it
came specifically from his experience and

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.630
his soul.

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:42.700
And it took another two years of writing everyday
before he could write something again that

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:43.700
was uniquely his.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:49.400
And then he got to where he could do it again
and again and again and he became Ray Bradbury

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:51.150
because he was determined to do that.

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:56.080
I thought that was a great lesson to say ‘Okay,
what are you creating?

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:00.530
What can you create that isn’t something
of what others are doing, but that is unique

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:04.970
to you, that no one else could have created,
that you create something fresh in the world

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:10.140
that’s truthful and meaningful and then
you know you’ve done something worth doing.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:12.580
So that’s been a great inspiration to me.

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:15.334
Elaine Zicree: There are so many people, they
are afraid of being hurt that they do the

00:03:15.334 --> 00:03:16.920
safe thing.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.220
So that if somebody says “That’s not very
good.”

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:20.220
They can say “I know.

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:21.220
It’s a piece of trash.

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:22.350
I was just doing what everybody else was doing.”

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:25.290
So you do put yourself out for far greater
vulnerability.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.240
Marc Zicree: With something original.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:31.990
Elaine Zicree: With something original, but
then to not do so is slowly soul killing.

00:03:31.990 --> 00:03:33.010
Marc Zicree: Yes.

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:34.020
Say that quickly.

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:36.370
Elaine Zicree: I failed to say it quickly.

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:38.069
I fell all over myself.

00:03:38.069 --> 00:03:39.340
[Laughs] It is.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:42.300
It’s like being worn down by the ocean.

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:44.209
Secretly it just does.

00:03:44.209 --> 00:03:48.849
So one of the things (as an example) when
we work together for TV, if we got a note,

00:03:48.849 --> 00:03:53.150
it doesn't matter if it’s a dumb note or
not (sometimes the notes were great, but sometimes

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:57.990
they were dumb) but we made pact that no matter
how dumb the note felt initially we would

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:03.120
not hand in that draft until we were able
to work that note in, in a way that made it

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:04.120
better.

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:08.090
Marc Zicree: Every draft had to be better
than the previous draft and so no matter how

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:10.680
challenging the notes were, we would find
a way to do that.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:15.000
And my friend Doug Heyes who was one of the
great directors of THE TWILIGHT ZONE, he did

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:18.889
Eye of the Beholder, The After Hours, and
The Howling Man and many other episodes, he

00:04:18.889 --> 00:04:24.660
was also a very good writer and he said “When
you get notes, the executives giving the notes

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:29.219
are often not writers and so the note (the
solution) they’re offering may not work.

00:04:29.219 --> 00:04:31.029
But there’s a concern under the note.

00:04:31.029 --> 00:04:34.050
There is something that is not working for
them and what they’re saying the solution

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:38.449
is may not be the right solution and never
put something in your script that doesn’t

00:04:38.449 --> 00:04:39.449
work.

00:04:39.449 --> 00:04:40.449
But you have to look at what the concern is.

00:04:40.449 --> 00:04:43.210
You have to figure out what the concern is
and address that.

00:04:43.210 --> 00:04:46.870
And if you come up with an alternative that
does work, they’ll be satisfied even if

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:50.580
the solution they offered isn’t the one
that you utilize as long as the concern is

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:53.770
addressed and that is a great piece of wisdom.

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:58.069
Elaine Zicree: You really want to maintain
a pride of workmanship and not just say “Oh

00:04:58.069 --> 00:04:59.889
well, you know” and just toss it off.

00:04:59.889 --> 00:05:01.650
First of all you don’t do that to the audience.

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:02.650
Marc Zicree: Oh no.

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:03.650
You give it your best no matter what.

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:07.080
Elaine Zicree: Because it represents you and
you make it represent you and you see it on

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:10.830
people on like assembly lines where you develop
something you don’t care about versus something

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:15.860
you care about and take a little bit of pride
in what you do and which I think is just critical

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:17.839
to the way that humans work.

00:05:17.839 --> 00:05:25.349
And yes we put up that fight…I always remember
when we were teaching there was a woman who

00:05:25.349 --> 00:05:26.349
shared with me.

00:05:26.349 --> 00:05:32.089
She had an action piece (the man, woman into
action thing) and it was like non-stop action,

00:05:32.089 --> 00:05:33.089
non-stop action.

00:05:33.089 --> 00:05:37.849
Going and going and going and the woman said
(who was the executive) “Well it sort of

00:05:37.849 --> 00:05:38.849
drags in the second act.

00:05:38.849 --> 00:05:40.589
It really drags.”

00:05:40.589 --> 00:05:44.370
And she flared but enough to keep it to herself
and she said “Well let me look at that.”

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:48.470
(that it was dragging in the second act) and
said how could it drag because there was non-stop

00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:49.479
action.

00:05:49.479 --> 00:05:53.460
And then it hit her on the way home that it
wasn’t that it was non-stop action that

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:59.809
in Act 1, she had not set up for us caring
sufficiently about the leads and the outcome

00:05:59.809 --> 00:06:05.270
for those leads for us to care about what
kind of threat they were facing because we

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:06.759
didn’t care.

00:06:06.759 --> 00:06:12.199
And so she said okay, well she’s going to
like deepen the characters and much more focus

00:06:12.199 --> 00:06:15.169
on their need and our investment in their
journey.

00:06:15.169 --> 00:06:20.490
She’s going to slow down the second act,
still keeping it exciting, but she’s going

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:23.800
to slow it down so we really know what the
struggle is about.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:28.559
The reason that it seemed slow was that we
just didn’t care.

00:06:28.559 --> 00:06:32.020
The executive then said “This is much better.

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:33.470
Now it really rolls along.”

00:06:33.470 --> 00:06:38.949
Now she couldn’t analyze why she didn’t
care in the second act but she was dead on.

00:06:38.949 --> 00:06:42.639
By the second act we were getting restive
and simply didn’t care because we didn’t

00:06:42.639 --> 00:06:46.319
know who was in the struggle.

00:06:46.319 --> 00:06:48.289
And so why would we care?

00:06:48.289 --> 00:06:51.029
Film Courage: So sometimes [the note giver]
doesn’t communicate what’s really going

00:06:51.029 --> 00:06:53.259
on, they just know that the story is falling
flat?

00:06:53.259 --> 00:06:54.259
Elaine Zicree: Yes.

00:06:54.259 --> 00:06:59.089
Marc Zicree: It’s not their responsibility
to make the script work, it’s your responsibility.

00:06:59.089 --> 00:07:07.710
And so they’ve hired you because you’re
a professional writer.

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:08.830
Executives get a bad rap.

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:11.880
Most of them are very smart and very talented
and very driven and they’re in the business

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:13.990
because they love it.

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:15.539
Everyone wants a script to work.

00:07:15.539 --> 00:07:17.039
Everyone wants a movie to work.

00:07:17.039 --> 00:07:21.599
Nobody wants to create something that fails,
but it’s very challenging.

00:07:21.599 --> 00:07:25.789
I mean, it’s hard to make anything good.

00:07:25.789 --> 00:07:29.180
But if you decide that you’re going to give
everything your best effort.

00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:33.229
That you’re going to bring everything you’ve
got to every job, that’s a good starting

00:07:33.229 --> 00:07:34.279
point.

00:07:34.279 --> 00:07:36.580
That’s been true in my whole career.

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:42.240
I started in animation and whether it was
writing SMURFS or SPACE COMMAND (and I don’t

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:46.080
just write things with an ’S’ starting
in the title).

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:50.589
But you say Okay, I’m going to bring everything
I’ve got to this and that gives you a certain

00:07:50.589 --> 00:07:51.919
professional integrity.

00:07:51.919 --> 00:07:58.189
Elaine Zicree: And the executives they’re
paid to get a sense of the story but you’re

00:07:58.189 --> 00:08:04.210
paid because you have the analysis in your
brain to say “Okay, there is something wrong.

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:08.129
I have to figure out what is wrong (specifically)”
but they’ve picked up that something is

00:08:08.129 --> 00:08:09.309
wrong and you’re probably accurate.

00:08:09.309 --> 00:08:13.629
Marc Zicree: But sometimes that is why it’s
good to have friends who are very talented

00:08:13.629 --> 00:08:16.099
so that if you’re stuck, you can call them
and say “Can we just bat this thing around

00:08:16.099 --> 00:08:17.099
a little bit?

00:08:17.099 --> 00:08:18.979
I’ve got to figure this out.”

00:08:18.979 --> 00:08:21.710
And Elaine is my sounding board.

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:23.839
Sometimes it will be like “Okay, this works.

00:08:23.839 --> 00:08:25.129
But how can we make this better.

00:08:25.129 --> 00:08:26.240
How can we make it go deeper?

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:27.380
How can it be more meaningful?”

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:29.060
And that’s very important.

